    {
      "id": "ea6da0f09967-q5",
      "number": 5,
      "question": "In a triangle ABC sides AB = 28 cm, BC = 32 cm and AC = 20 cm. AD is the angle bisector of &ang;A on BC and AM is the median of the same side. The distance between the foot of median and the foot of angle bisector is",
      "questionHtml": "In a triangle ABC sides AB = 28 cm, BC = 32 cm and AC = 20 cm. AD is the angle bisector of &ang;A on BC and AM is the median of the same side. The distance between the foot of median and the foot of angle bisector is",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "1 cm",
          "html": "1 cm"
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "8/3 cm",
          "html": "8/3&nbsp;<span style=\"\">cm&nbsp;</span>"
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "7/3 cm",
          "html": "7/3&nbsp;<span style=\"\">cm&nbsp;</span>"
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "2 cm",
          "html": "2&nbsp;<span style=\"\">cm</span>"
        }
      ],
      "correctAnswer": "B",
      "solution": "<span style='display: flex;gap: 6px;align-items: center;'><img height='26px' src='//cdn.testbook.com/resources/lms_creative_elements/shortcut-trick-image.png' width='26px' /> <u style='font-size: 21px; font-weight: 700; display: inline !important;'>Shortcut Trick</u></span>\n\n<p>By standard formula, the distance between the foot of the median and the foot of the angle bisector on side <strong>BC</strong> is given by:</p>\n<p><strong>DM = |(BC ÷ 2) − [AB × BC ÷ (AB + AC)]|</strong></p>\n<p>Substitute the given values: <strong>AB = 28 cm</strong>, <strong>AC = 20 cm</strong>, and <strong>BC = 32 cm</strong>:</p>\n<p>⇒ DM = |16 − [28 × 32 ÷ 48]|</p>\n<p>⇒ DM = |16 − 56/3| = 8/3 cm</p>\n<p><strong>∴ The correct answer is 8/3 cm.</strong></p>\n\n<p><span style='display: flex;gap: 6px;align-items: center;'><img height='26px' src='//cdn.testbook.com/resources/lms_creative_elements/alternate-methord-image.png' width='26px' /><strong><span style='vertical-align: middle;font-size: 21px;'><u>Alternate Method</u></span></strong></span></p>\n\n<p><strong>Given:</strong></p>\n<p>Sides of triangle ABC: <strong>AB = 28 cm</strong>, <strong>BC = 32 cm</strong>, and <strong>AC = 20 cm</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>AD</strong> is the angle bisector of ∠A on BC and <strong>AM</strong> is the median to BC.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Formula Used:</strong></p>\n<p>1. Midpoint property: <strong>BM = MC = BC ÷ 2</strong></p>\n<p>2. Angle Bisector Theorem: <strong>BD ÷ DC = AB ÷ AC</strong></p>\n\n<div style=\"text-align: left;\"><img src=\"https://cdn.testbook.com/tb_solImg_8bdd8711.png/1784542168.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto;\"></div>\n\n<p><strong>Calculations:</strong></p>\n<p>Since <strong>AM</strong> is the median to <strong>BC</strong>, <strong>M</strong> is the midpoint of <strong>BC</strong>:</p>\n<p>⇒ BM = BC ÷ 2</p>\n<p>⇒ BM = 32 ÷ 2 = 16 cm</p>\n<p>According to the <strong>Angle Bisector Theorem</strong>, the bisector <strong>AD</strong> divides the opposite side <strong>BC</strong> in the ratio of the other two sides <strong>AB</strong> and <strong>AC</strong>:</p>\n<p>⇒ BD ÷ DC = AB ÷ AC</p>\n<p>⇒ BD ÷ DC = 28 ÷ 20 = 7 ÷ 5</p>\n<p>Let BD = 7k and DC = 5k.</p>\n<p>⇒ BD + DC = BC</p>\n<p>⇒ 7k + 5k = 32</p>\n<p>⇒ 12k = 32</p>\n<p>⇒ k = 32 ÷ 12 = 8/3</p>\n<p>Now, find the length of <strong>BD</strong>:</p>\n<p>⇒ BD = 7 × (8/3) = 56/3 cm</p>\n<p>The distance between the foot of the median (<strong>M</strong>) and the foot of the angle bisector (<strong>D</strong>) is <strong>DM</strong>:</p>\n<p>⇒ DM = BD − BM</p>\n<p>⇒ DM = 56/3 − 16</p>\n<p>⇒ DM = (56 − 48) ÷ 3</p>\n<p>⇒ DM = 8/3 cm</p>\n<p><strong>∴ The correct answer is 8/3 cm.</strong></p>\n\n<p><span style='display: flex;gap: 6px;align-items: center;'><img height='26px' src='//cdn.testbook.com/resources/lms_creative_elements/additional-information-image.png' width='26px' /><strong><span style='vertical-align: middle;font-size: 21px;'><u>Additional Information</u></span></strong></span></p>\n\n<p style='margin:8px 0 2px 0;font-weight:700;color:#222222;'>Angle Bisector Theorem</p>\n<p style='margin:0 0 8px 0;color:#444444;'>An angle bisector of an angle of a triangle divides the opposite side into two segments that are proportional to the other two sides of the triangle.</p>\n\n<p style='margin:8px 0 2px 0;font-weight:700;color:#222222;'>Median of a Triangle</p>\n<p style='margin:0 0 8px 0;color:#444444;'>A median of a triangle is a line segment joining a vertex to the midpoint of the opposite side, thus bisecting that side into two equal halves.</p>\n\n<p style='margin:8px 0 2px 0;font-weight:700;color:#222222;'>Length of Angle Bisector (Stewart's Theorem)</p>\n<p style='margin:0 0 8px 0;color:#444444;'>The length of the angle bisector AD can be calculated using the formula: AD<sup>2</sup> = AB × AC − BD × DC.</p>",
      "images": [
        "//cdn.testbook.com/resources/lms_creative_elements/shortcut-trick-image.png",
        "//cdn.testbook.com/resources/lms_creative_elements/alternate-methord-image.png",
        "https://cdn.testbook.com/tb_solImg_8bdd8711.png/1784542168.png",
        "//cdn.testbook.com/resources/lms_creative_elements/additional-information-image.png"
      ],
      "subject": null,
      "topic": null,
      "difficulty": null
    },
    {
      "id": "ea6da0f09967-q6",
      "number": 6,
      "question": "In the figure given below: QM, PN, and RT are the altitudes of an isosceles triangle and PQ = QR = 15 PR = 18 cm. Find the ratio of QT/QO.",
      "questionHtml": "<p>In the figure given below: QM, PN, and RT are the altitudes of an isosceles triangle and PQ = QR = 15&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"https://storage.googleapis.com/tb-img/production/22/09/F4_Madhuri_SSC_28.09.2022_D8.png\" /></p>\n\n<p style=\"\">PR = 18 cm.&nbsp;Find the ratio of QT/QO.</p>\n",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "4 : 5",
          "html": "4 : 5"
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "3 : 5",
          "html": "3 : 5"
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "3 : 4",
          "html": "3 : 4"
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "2 : 5",
          "html": "2 : 5"
        }
      ],
      "correctAnswer": "A",
      "solution": "<p><strong>Given:</strong></p>\n\n<p>&Delta;PQR is an isosceles triangle and QM, PN, and RT are the altitudes of the triangle</p>\n\n<p>PQ = QR = 15 cm</p>\n\n<p>PR = 18 cm</p>\n\n<p><strong>Concept used:</strong></p>\n\n<p>Two sides of an isosceles triangle are equal</p>\n\n<p>The altitude of an isosceles triangle is the perpendicular bisector of the non-equal side.</p>\n\n<p>Pythagoras theorem:&nbsp;In a right-angled triangle, the square of the hypotenuse side is equal to the sum of squares of the other two sides</p>\n\n<p>If two triangles are similar then the ratio of their corresponding sides and angles are also equal.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Calculation:</strong></p>\n\n<p><img src=\"https://storage.googleapis.com/tb-img/production/22/09/F4_Madhuri_SSC_28.09.2022_D8.png\" style=\"\" /></p>\n\n<p>In&nbsp;&Delta;PQM, PM = PR/2 = 18/2 = 9 cm (∵ QM is the altitude of isosceles &Delta;PQR)</p>\n\n<p>QM<sup>2</sup>&nbsp;= PQ<sup>2</sup>&nbsp;- PM<sup>2</sup></p>\n\n<p>&rArr; QM<sup>2</sup>&nbsp;= 15<sup>2</sup>&nbsp;-&nbsp;9<sup>2</sup>&nbsp;= 144</p>\n\n<p>&rArr; QM =&nbsp;&radic;144 = 12 cm</p>\n\n<p>In&nbsp;&Delta;QTO and&nbsp;&Delta;QMP,</p>\n\n<p>&ang;QTO =&nbsp;&ang;QMP = 90&deg;&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>&ang;TQO&nbsp;<span style=\"\">=&nbsp; &ang;MQP&nbsp;</span> (∵ angles between the same lines)</p>\n\n<p>Hence,&nbsp;&Delta;QTO&nbsp;&sim;&nbsp;&Delta;QMP</p>\n\n<p>&there4; QT/QO = QM/QP (∵ ratio of the corresponding sides of the similar triangle are equal)&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>&rArr; 12/15 = 4/5</p>\n\n<p><strong>&there4; QT/QO = 4/5</strong></p>\n",
      "images": [
        "https://storage.googleapis.com/tb-img/production/22/09/F4_Madhuri_SSC_28.09.2022_D8.png"
      ],
      "subject": null,
      "topic": null,
      "difficulty": null
    },
    {
      "id": "ea6da0f09967-q7",
      "number": 7,
      "question": "In &Delta;PRS, the base RS of the triangle &Delta;PRS is extended to point T. The internal angle bisector of &ang;R and external angle bisector of &ang;S intersects at point Q. If &ang;RQS = 30 ∘ and &ang;PSQ = 60 ∘ , then find the &ang;PRQ.",
      "questionHtml": "In&nbsp;&Delta;PRS, the base RS of the triangle&nbsp;<span style=\"\">&Delta;PRS</span> is extended to point T. The internal angle bisector of &ang;R and external angle bisector of &ang;S intersects at point Q. If&nbsp;&ang;RQS = 30<sup>∘</sup>&nbsp;and&nbsp;&ang;PSQ = 60<sup>∘</sup>, then find the&nbsp;&ang;PRQ.",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "15 ∘",
          "html": "<span style=\"\">15</span><span style=\" line-height: 0; position: relative; vertical-align: baseline; top: -0.5em; font-size:10.5px;\">∘</span>"
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "30 ∘",
          "html": "<span style=\"\">30</span><span style=\" line-height: 0; position: relative; vertical-align: baseline; top: -0.5em; font-size:10.5px;\">∘</span>"
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "60 ∘",
          "html": "<span style=\"\">60</span><span style=\" line-height: 0; position: relative; vertical-align: baseline; top: -0.5em; font-size:10.5px;\">∘</span>"
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "40 ∘",
          "html": "<span style=\"\">40</span><span style=\" line-height: 0; position: relative; vertical-align: baseline; top: -0.5em; font-size:10.5px;\">∘</span>"
        }
      ],
      "correctAnswer": "B",
      "solution": "<p><strong>Given:</strong></p>\n\n<p><span style=\"\">In&nbsp;&Delta;PRS, the base RS of the triangle&nbsp;</span><span style=\"\">&Delta;PRS</span><span style=\"\">&nbsp;is extended to point T.</span></p>\n\n<p><span style=\"\">The angle bisector of &ang;R and angle bisector of &ang;S intersects at point Q.</span></p>\n\n<p><span style=\"\">&ang;RQS = 30</span><span style=\" line-height: 0; position: relative; vertical-align: baseline; top: -0.5em; font-size:10.5px;\">∘</span><span style=\"\">&nbsp;and&nbsp;&ang;PSQ = 60</span><span style=\" line-height: 0; position: relative; vertical-align: baseline; top: -0.5em; font-size:10.5px;\">∘</span><span style=\"\">,</span></p>\n\n<p><strong>Concept used:</strong></p>\n\n<p><span style=\"\">(1.) straight angle subtends 180</span><span style=\" line-height: 0; position: relative; vertical-align: baseline; top: -0.5em; font-size:10.5px;\">∘</span>.</p>\n\n<p>(2.) Sum of all internal angles of a triangle is&nbsp;<span style=\"\">180</span><span style=\" line-height: 0; position: relative; vertical-align: baseline; top: -0.5em; font-size:10.5px;\">∘</span>.</p>\n\n<p><span style=\"\"><strong>Calculation:</strong></span></p>\n\n<p><span style=\"\">According to the question, the required image is:</span></p>\n\n<p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://storage.googleapis.com/tb-img/production/22/09/F4_Madhuri_SSC_28.09.2022_D15.png\" style=\"width: 254px; height: 162px;\" /></p>\n\n<p>Since QS is an external angle bisector of&nbsp;&ang;PST,</p>\n\n<p>Therefore,&nbsp;&ang;PSQ =&nbsp;&ang;QST = <span style=\"\">60</span><span style=\" line-height: 0; position: relative; vertical-align: baseline; top: -0.5em; font-size:10.5px;\">∘</span></p>\n\n<p>Since straight angle subtends 180<sup>∘</sup>,</p>\n\n<p>Therefore,&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>&rArr;&nbsp;&ang;PSR +&nbsp;<span style=\"\">&ang;PSQ +&nbsp;&ang;QST =&nbsp;180</span><span style=\" line-height: 0; position: relative; vertical-align: baseline; top: -0.5em; font-size:10.5px;\">∘</span></p>\n\n<p style=\"\">&rArr;&nbsp;&ang;PSR +&nbsp;<span style=\"\">60</span><span style=\" line-height: 0; position: relative; vertical-align: baseline; top: -0.5em; font-size:10.5px;\">∘</span> +&nbsp;<span style=\"\">60</span><span style=\" line-height: 0; position: relative; vertical-align: baseline; top: -0.5em; font-size:10.5px;\">∘</span> =&nbsp;180<span style=\"line-height: 0; position: relative; vertical-align: baseline; top: -0.5em; font-size:10.5px;\">∘</span></p>\n\n<p>&rArr;&nbsp;&ang;PSR + 120<span style=\"line-height: 0; position: relative; vertical-align: baseline; top: -0.5em; font-size:10.5px;\">∘</span>&nbsp;=&nbsp;180<span style=\"line-height: 0; position: relative; vertical-align: baseline; top: -0.5em; font-size:10.5px;\">∘</span></p>\n\n<p style=\"\">&rArr;&nbsp;&ang;PSR = 60<span style=\"line-height: 0; position: relative; vertical-align: baseline; top: -0.5em; font-size:10.5px;\">∘</span></p>\n\n<p>Now,</p>\n\n<p>&rArr;&nbsp;&ang;QSR =&nbsp;<span style=\"\">&ang;QSP +&nbsp;&ang;PSR =&nbsp;120</span><span style=\" line-height: 0; position: relative; vertical-align: baseline; top: -0.5em; font-size:10.5px;\">∘</span></p>\n\n<p>In&nbsp;&Delta;RSQ,</p>\n\n<p>&rArr; &ang;QRS +&nbsp;<span style=\"\">&ang;QSR +&nbsp;&ang;RQS =&nbsp;180</span><span style=\" line-height: 0; position: relative; vertical-align: baseline; top: -0.5em; font-size:10.5px;\">∘</span></p>\n\n<p style=\"\">&rArr; &ang;QRS + 120<sup>∘</sup>&nbsp;+ 3<span style=\"\">0</span><span style=\" line-height: 0; position: relative; vertical-align: baseline; top: -0.5em; font-size:10.5px;\">∘</span>&nbsp;=&nbsp;180<span style=\"line-height: 0; position: relative; vertical-align: baseline; top: -0.5em; font-size:10.5px;\">∘</span></p>\n\n<p style=\"\">&rArr; &ang;QRS = 30<span style=\"line-height: 0; position: relative; vertical-align: baseline; top: -0.5em; font-size:10.5px;\">∘</span></p>\n\n<div>Since QR is an internal angle bisector for of&nbsp;&ang;R,</div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;\n<p style=\"\">Therefore,&nbsp;&ang;PRQ =&nbsp;&ang;QRS =&nbsp;<span style=\"\">30</span><span style=\" line-height: 0; position: relative; vertical-align: baseline; top: -0.5em; font-size:10.5px;\">∘</span></p>\n</div>\n\n<div><strong>Therefore, &#39;</strong><span style=\"\">30</span><span style=\" line-height: 0; position: relative; vertical-align: baseline; top: -0.5em; font-size:10.5px;\">∘</span><strong>&#39; is the required answer.</strong></div>\n",
